## Blocking finding

ADV-P3-01: a duplicate picture-list row id corrupts the manifest.

- The writer refuses a duplicate id in the approval list at
  `src/storytime/muse-delivery.ts:341-348`. It does not refuse a duplicate id in `options.rows`.
- `assembleManifest` pairs candidates by `candidate.jobId === job.id` at
  `src/storytime/book-pictures.ts:2081-2084`.
- Live probe: two rows with the id `l2-w01-story-page-a-nut` (file names
  `l2-w01-story-page-a-nut-v1.png` and `l2-w01-story-page-a-nut-b-v1.png`) and one approval entry
  give 2 jobs. Each job holds candidates `[l2-w01-story-page-a-nut-v1.png,
  l2-w01-story-page-a-nut-b-v1.png]` and selects `l2-w01-story-page-a-nut-v1.png`. The folder holds
  4 delivered files. The second row has no job that selects its own file.
- Fix: refuse a duplicate `row.id` in `options.rows` before the delivered-name loop, in the same
  way as the approval-list check at `src/storytime/muse-delivery.ts:343`.
- Failing test: `tests/storytime-muse-delivery-adversarial.test.ts:567`.

## Nonblocking finding

ADV-P3-NB1 (Low): a symlinked delivery root is followed. `resolveMuseDeliveryPaths` confines the
paths by `path.resolve` only (`src/storytime/muse-delivery.ts:130-146`), and the writer creates and
joins the delivery root with no realpath check (`src/storytime/muse-delivery.ts:461-478`). A live
probe wrote 3 files through a directory symlink to a folder outside the lexical out tree. The writer
still refuses a symlink at every file target. A local actor must create the link, so the impact is
low.

## Notes

- RB11-01 is stale on its 1e21 example. The schema refuses 1e21 with "Too big: expected int to be
  <=9007199254740991" at `entries.0.fileVersion`. ADV11 proves the refusal. This run does not
  re-report RB11-01.
- The writer copies the print that the approval entry names. No spec or plan rule asks it to check
  the row id inside the Muse file name, so ADV1 records that boundary as an approval-list authoring
  error, not a writer defect.
- Two first runs of the new file failed to transform, because two `expect.soft` object literals
  were missing a closing brace. These were test-authoring errors. The fixes changed the new test
  file only.
- No production mutation ran. The role must not edit `src/`, and ADV9 fails on its own, so the
  suite proves that it can fail. ADV3 and ADV11 each hold a passing control beside the refusal case.

